  "content": {
    "title": "Didn’t get a Drop 1 time slot? Here’s what happens next for LA28 Olympic ticket hopefuls",
    "text": "by: Iman Palm Posted: Apr 7, 2026 / 11:44 AM PDT Updated: Apr 7, 2026 / 11:49 AM PDT LA28 Olympic ticket notifications have begun rolling out, and many fans are now finding out whether they’ve been selected for the first general sales window. The initial public ticket drop, known as Drop 1, runs from April 9–19 and assigns purchase windows through a random lottery. If you were assigned a time slot, here’s what to expect. Registrants will receive an email on Tuesday with one of two outcomes: either a designated date and time for their 48‑hour purchase window, or a notice that they were not selected for Drop 1. Anyone who does not receive a time slot is automatically entered into Drop 2, and no additional registration or action is required, according to LA28 officials. How Purchase Windows Work Selected fans get a 48‑hour window to buy tickets. Once a ticket is added to the cart, buyers have 30 minutes to check out. Multiple transactions are allowed within the 48‑hour window until the ticket maximum is reached. Tickets are sold by category; seat assignments come closer to the Games, consistent with Olympic ticketing procedures. Ticket Limits Fans may purchase: Up to 12 Olympic event tickets Up to 12 Football (Soccer) Tournament tickets (not counted toward the general 12‑ticket cap) Up to 4 tickets per Opening or Closing Ceremony, which do count toward the 12‑ticket Olympic maximum Tickets will be available across all sports, including the Opening and Closing Ceremonies. How much are tickets? Ticket prices vary widely, with entry-level seats starting at about $28 and premium experiences exceeding $1,000 depending on the sport, session and seat location. According to LA28, roughly half of all tickets are expected to cost more than $200, and about 5% will cost more than $1,000. During the locals’ presale, which ended Monday, many Angelenos said they were surprised by the final totals at checkout. Some opted out after seeing higher‑than‑expected prices or encountering steep service fees, up to 24%, as reported by LAist Future Draws and Paralympic Tickets Fans who do not receive a time slot for the locals presale or Drop 1 will automatically be entered into future draws. Registration is one‑time only; fans remain eligible for all subsequent drops. Paralympic tickets will go on sale in 2027.",
